# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_TRACE_CONFIGURE */
int64_t trace_configure(uint32_t tracing, uint32_t op, uint64_t a, uint64_t b,
                        uint64_t c, uint64_t sample_hz);
```

# DESCRIPTION

The kernel records events (context switches, interrupts, system calls, channel messages and the rest) into one ring per CPU.
`trace_configure` starts and stops the recording and hands out the rings.
It takes six arguments, so it is made with `croi_syscall6`.
<tracing> is a resource(2) that grants the TRACING system resource; every operation needs it.
<op> is one of:

: `CROI_TRACE_OP_START`
  Starts recording the categories in <a>, discarding what was recorded.
  <b> is the size of each CPU's ring in pages, from 1 to 4096, rounded up to a power of two; a page holds `CROI_TRACE_RECORDS_PER_PAGE` (128) records.
  <c> is the mode: `CROI_TRACE_ONESHOT` stops recording on a CPU when its ring is full and counts the drops; `CROI_TRACE_CIRCULAR` overwrites the oldest.
  <sample_hz> is the rate of the tick sampler, if `CROI_TRACE_SAMPLE` is on, in samples a second of each CPU's busy time (time it runs a thread other than its idle one, however short its stretches); 0 means `CROI_SAMPLE_DEFAULT_HZ` (1000).
  A bad <b>, <c> or <sample_hz> is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`; no memory for the rings is `CROI_ERR_NO_MEMORY`.
: `CROI_TRACE_OP_STOP`
  Stops recording; it returns when no CPU is part way through a record.
: `CROI_TRACE_OP_REWIND`
  Stops, and empties the rings.
: `CROI_TRACE_OP_MARK`
  Records a mark of 16 bytes, <a> and <b>, of the caller's choosing (kind `CROI_TK_MARK`), if the mark category is on.
: `CROI_TRACE_OP_RINGS`
  Writes a handle to each CPU's ring to the array of `uint32_t` at <a>, whose capacity <b> must be at least the number of CPUs (`CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`), and returns the number of handles.
  Each handle is a VMO with READ, MAP and GET_PROPERTY, the basic rights too, and no WRITE.
  Before the first START there are no rings (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`).

Any other <op> is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

The categories are bits of <a>:

| Category | Bit | Records |
| `CROI_TRACE_SCHED` | 0 | switches, wakeups, blocks, preemptions, migrations, overruns |
| `CROI_TRACE_IRQ` | 1 | interrupt entry and exit |
| `CROI_TRACE_VM` | 2 | page faults and commits |
| `CROI_TRACE_IPC` | 3 | channel writes and reads, and the donation of a call |
| `CROI_TRACE_FUTEX` | 4 | futex waits and wakes |
| `CROI_TRACE_SYSCALL` | 5 | the entry and exit of every system call |
| `CROI_TRACE_SAMPLE` | 6 | samples of the running thread's program counter and frames |
| `CROI_TRACE_MARK` | 7 | marks |

A ring is a `croi_trace_ring_t` header of 128 bytes (counter frequency, session, drops, mode) followed at `CROI_TRACE_RING_HEADER` by a ring of the format of ring.h, overwrite mode, format `CROI_TRACE_RING_FORMAT`.
A reader maps it and reads it as any ring; it stops the trace first for a steady view.
Each slot is a `croi_trace_slot_t`, the ring's sequence number and a record of 32 bytes:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint64_t time;     // the counter's raw value; the header has its Hz
  uint16_t kind;
  uint16_t cpu;
  uint32_t thread;   // task << 12 | thread index; the kernel's are task 0
  uint64_t a;
  uint64_t b;
} croi_trace_record_t;
```

The kernel's kinds are below 0x4000; 0x4000 to 0x7fff belong to user space's own trace rings, so the two merge into one timeline.
object_get_info(2) `CROI_INFO_THREAD_TRACE` gives the id a thread is named by.
The flow id that joins the write and the read of a channel message is `croi_flow_id` in `<croi/syscall.h>`.

